+// wasip2 / conservative-GC workaround. JSC's GC finds C++ roots by
+// conservatively scanning the machine stack + registers. On wasm32 the
+// "registers" are the wasm value-stack locals, which are NOT in linear memory
+// and cannot be scanned (StackBounds only covers the shadow/data stack). A
+// JSCell* held only in such a local across a GC-triggering allocation is
+// therefore invisible and can be swept while still live.
+//
+// A structure transition (`obj.prop = x` adding a property) hits this: while
+// `Structure::addNewPropertyTransition` allocates the *new* Structure, the base
+// (`this`) Structure is live only through an optimized local, so an eden GC can
+// reclaim it — leaving the object with a dangling structure that reads back as
+// garbage (e.g. `typeof` of a fresh once-wrapper closure returning "object",
+// which broke node:http's `listen`).
+//
+// At -O0, clang keeps locals in `alloca`s on the (scannable) shadow stack
+// instead of promoting them to wasm locals via mem2reg, so the conservative
+// scan finds them. Disabling optimization for this TU is a targeted, correct
+// (if slower) fix — the file's hot property-lookup fast paths are inlined from
+// StructureInlines.h into callers and are unaffected; only out-of-line
+// transition/table code (rare after shapes stabilize) is slowed. The proper
+// long-term fix is precise GC roots for the wasm target.
+#if defined(__wasi__)
+#pragma clang optimize off
+#endif
